<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <parent>
        <groupId>com.ttg</groupId>
        <artifactId>dev-knowledge-platform</artifactId>
        <version>0.0.1-SNAPSHOT</version>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>gateway</artifactId>
    <name>Gateway</name>
    <description>Spring Boot entry point, security/JWT wiring, and Liquibase migrations — zero embedded feature modules, zero REST controllers of its own</description>

    <dependencies>

        <!-- Internal -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.ttg</groupId>
            <artifactId>common</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.ttg</groupId>
            <artifactId>infra</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- No identity-service dependency: it's a standalone Spring Boot app now (its own
             `identity` schema, own JWT verification, own port), not wired into this Spring
             context — see the project-microservices-extraction-plan memory / root CLAUDE.md.
             This app now JIT-provisions/refreshes its own local `product.USER` row directly
             (security/KeycloakJwtAuthenticationConverter), duplicated from identity-service's
             equivalent rather than shared, same reasoning as ecommerce-service's copy below.
             gateway will reach identity-service over HTTP once the proxy layer is built — not
             done yet. Do not re-add this as a Maven dependency; that would put its beans back on
             this app's classpath and cause both apps to run them at once.

             No ecommerce-service dependency either, for the same reason: it's a standalone Spring
             Boot app now (its own database, own JWT verification, own port), not wired into this
             Spring context. gateway will reach it over HTTP once the proxy layer is built — not
             done yet, see docs/CHANGELOG.md. Do not re-add this as a Maven dependency; that would
             put its beans (OutboxRelay, controllers, etc.) back on this app's classpath and cause
             both apps to run them at once.

             No task-service dependency either, same reasoning again: it's a standalone Spring Boot
             app now (its own `task` schema, own JWT verification, own port 8083), not wired into
             this Spring context. Unlike identity-service/ecommerce-service, this app never called
             into task-service's Java classes in-process to begin with (ProjectApi/TaskApi were
             already that module's own REST layer, just riding on this app's Spring context via the
             Maven dependency) — so nothing needed rewriting here, this was a pure pom.xml removal.
             gateway will reach task-service over HTTP once the proxy layer is built — not done yet.
             Do not re-add this as a Maven dependency; that would put its beans back on this app's
             classpath and cause both apps to run them at once.

             No social-service dependency either: it's a standalone Spring Boot app now (its own
             `social` schema, own JWT verification, own port 8084), not wired into this Spring
             context. Unlike identity-service/ecommerce-service, this app never called into
             social-service's Java classes in-process to begin with (FriendApi/GroupApi/DmApi/
             UserApi were already that module's own REST layer, just riding on this app's Spring
             context via the Maven dependency) — so the only rewriting needed here was
             DataSeedingRunner (no longer seeds the friend graph/blocks/DMs — see that class's
             Javadoc) and removing this app's own WebSocketConfig/StompAuthChannelInterceptor/
             CurrentUserIdMessageArgumentResolver entirely, since chat was the only STOMP use case
             in this reactor and social-service now owns that whole transport on its own port.
             gateway will reach social-service over HTTP once the proxy layer is built — not done
             yet. Do not re-add this as a Maven dependency; that would put its beans back on this
             app's classpath and cause both apps to run them at once.

             No content-service dependency either: it's a standalone Spring Boot app now (its own
             `content` schema, own JWT verification, own port 8085), not wired into this Spring
             context. Unlike task-service/social-service, ai-service (still embedded here) had a
             real one-directional dependency on content-service (ContentEmbedding's FK,
             ContentIngestionService.ingest(...) taking a live ContentItem) — that was rewritten to
             a real HTTP call (ai-service's ContentServiceClient, app.content-service.* below) before
             this Maven dependency could be safely removed, unlike the other four extractions where
             the dependency was purely a pom.xml edit from day one. PublicContentApi/
             PublicContentController also moved back into content-service in the same extraction —
             they used to live here only because this app could see both content-service and
             ai-service, which stopped being a real constraint once ai-service already depended on
             content-service directly. gateway will reach content-service over HTTP once the proxy
             layer is built — not done yet. Do not re-add this as a Maven dependency; that would put
             its beans back on this app's classpath and cause both apps to run them at once.

             No ai-service dependency either — the sixth and final embedded feature module,
             extracted the same way as content-service (its own `ai` schema, own JWT verification,
             own port 8086, own claims-based KeycloakJwtAuthenticationConverter —
             ChatSession.userUuid/PipelineMetrics.userUuid are plain columns now, not a FK to
             product.USER). This app now has zero remaining embedded feature modules and zero REST
             controllers of its own — see root CLAUDE.md's Long-term direction section for what (if
             anything) comes next for this module. gateway will reach ai-service over HTTP once the
             proxy layer is built — not done yet. Do not re-add this as a Maven dependency; that
             would put its beans (sseStreamExecutor, the chat/ingestion/embedding-index/
             pipeline-metrics controllers, etc.) back on this app's classpath and cause both apps to
             run them at once. -->

        <!-- Spring Web -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Spring Boot Security -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Spring Boot OAuth2 Client (provides OidcUserService, OAuth2 login flow) -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-oauth2-client</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Spring Boot OAuth2 Resource Server -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- JWT (JJWT) -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.jsonwebtoken</groupId>
            <artifactId>jjwt-api</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.jsonwebtoken</groupId>
            <artifactId>jjwt-impl</artifactId>
            <scope>runtime</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.jsonwebtoken</groupId>
            <artifactId>jjwt-jackson</artifactId>
            <scope>runtime</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Actuator -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- PostgreSQL -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.postgresql</groupId>
            <artifactId>postgresql</artifactId>
            <scope>runtime</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!-- MinIO — S3-compatible object storage. This app has no code of its own that calls
             StorageService, but infra's StorageServiceImpl/StorageConfig (@Service/@Configuration,
             under com.ttg.devknowledgeplatform.infra.*) are component-scanned into this app's
             Spring context regardless — MinioClient must stay on the classpath for that bean to
             construct at startup. Do not remove without also excluding those beans. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.minio</groupId>
            <artifactId>minio</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Dev Tools -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-devtools</artifactId>
            <scope>runtime</scope>
            <optional>true</optional>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Docker Compose support -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-docker-compose</artifactId>
            <scope>runtime</scope>
            <optional>true</optional>
        </dependency>

    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
                <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                <configuration>
                    <excludes>
                        <exclude>
                            <groupId>org.projectlombok</groupId>
                            <artifactId>lombok</artifactId>
                        </exclude>
                    </excludes>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>
